  {
    num: '05',
    title: 'Waiting to feel 100% certain before starting any real test',
    body: "Career theorist John Krumboltz's research on planned happenstance found that most real careers are shaped substantially by unplanned events that people only benefit from if they stay curious, flexible, and willing to act under uncertainty. Waiting for total certainty before your first real test usually means waiting past the point where testing was still cheap.",
    fix: 'Do this instead: pick the option with the strongest current signal and run one small real test on it now, instead of waiting to feel sure about all of them at once.',
  },
  {
    num: '06',
    title: 'Checking the Market Signal at the field level instead of the exact-combination level',
    body: 'A field like "data" or "marketing" can look strong in national data while the exact combination you are testing - your specific tools, your specific city, your specific experience level - has almost no real demand yet. National employability numbers describe the whole market, not your narrow slice of it.',
    fix: 'Do this instead: run the 5-step Market Signal check below on the exact combination you are considering, not the broad field name.',
  },
];

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'What is the fastest way to find the right career for me?',
    a: 'There is no verdict you can get in one sitting. The fastest honest path is to gather the 4 Fit Signals - Attention, Feedback, Friction, and Market - in parallel: run a structured interest tool, ask people who know your work one direct feedback question, notice which tasks energise vs drain you, and check whether real employers pay for that exact combination right now. One short real test in a shortlisted lane moves you further than another quiz.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Are personality tests like MBTI or 16Personalities reliable for choosing a career?',
    a: 'Treat them as one soft input, not a verdict. Research on the Myers-Briggs style format has found that a large share of people receive a different four-letter type when they retake the same test within weeks, mainly because most people score near the middle of each scale. RIASEC-style interest tools and real skill feedback from other people are stronger evidence for a career decision than a personality label alone.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is a career aptitude test enough to decide my career?',
    a: 'An aptitude or interest test is a useful first data point, not the finish line. It can narrow a wide field fast, but it cannot tell you whether you can tolerate the daily task mix of a specific role, whether real employers pay for that exact combination right now, or whether the skill holds up once tested against a real deadline or a real difficult client.',
  },
  {
    q: 'I am in my late 20s or 30s and still do not know what I want to do - is it too late?',
    a: "No. LinkedIn's 2026 India data found professionals across every generation, from Gen Z to Baby Boomers, reporting similar confusion about career direction - this is not a problem unique to people who are behind. The 4 Fit Signals work the same way at any age: gather Attention, Feedback, Friction, and Market evidence about your current situation, then test one adjacent move before committing to a dramatic reset.",
  },
  {
    q: 'What if I like too many things and cannot narrow it down?',
    a: 'Test one interest cluster at a time instead of waiting to feel fully certain about all of them. Multi-passionate people who wait for total clarity often stay stuck the longest, because every option remains abstract. Pick the cluster with the strongest current Feedback or Market signal, run one small real test in it, then move to the next cluster if it does not hold up.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What if everyone tells me a field is booming but I do not feel drawn to it?',
    a: "A booming field label is only one piece of the Market Signal - it says nothing about your Attention or Friction fit. Non-technical graduate employability in India actually fell in Mercer Mettl's most recent data even as some fields kept growing, which shows that a growing field and a good personal fit are not the same claim. Check all four signals before switching direction based on a label alone.",
  },
  {
    q: 'How long does it take to find the right career for me?',
    a: "There is no fixed timeline, and treating it as a single deadline usually backfires. Some people gather strong evidence across all four signals within a short, focused stretch; others need a longer period of testing across a couple of different clusters. What matters is that you are running real, small tests during that stretch, not that you hit a specific week or month count.",
  },
  {
    q: 'Should I quit my current job to find the right career?',
    a: 'Usually not as the first move. Job-crafting research from organisational psychologists Amy Wrzesniewski and Jane Dutton found that people can meaningfully reshape the tasks, relationships, and meaning inside an existing role before ever needing to leave it. Test an adjacent proof piece inside or beside your current job first, and treat quitting as a later step once your signals clearly point elsewhere.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the difference between this and taking a career assessment?',
    a: 'A single assessment gives you one data point, usually for one signal - most often Attention or a personality label. This approach treats that assessment as useful input into a larger evidence set that also includes Feedback from real people, your own Friction pattern across real tasks, and a genuine real-world Market check, then asks you to test the combination in real life before committing years to it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I actually check whether real employers pay for my exact combination?',
    a: 'Run the 5-step Market Signal check: write the exact combination instead of a broad field name, pull 15-20 real job postings for it, check who is actually posting, cross-check the real pay range across a few sources, and message two people already doing the role. A field-level statistic cannot tell you this - only a check on your exact combination can.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What if my four signals point in completely different directions?',
    a: 'Read the pattern, not just the individual scores. Strong Attention and Feedback with a weak Market signal usually means a real strength that needs a narrower niche or a different packaging, not a wasted interest. Strong Market with weak Attention and Feedback is a paycheck path, usable as a bridge but risky as a lifelong bet. If two options both look reasonable after this, a structured comparison method like the 5-step decision ladder can help you choose between them.',
